     There are no optimizations or loops to mark


    1.          SUBROUTINE Q1DA(A,B,EPS,R,E,KF,F,IFLAG)
    2.    C***BEGIN PROLOGUE Q1DA
    3.    C***DATE WRITTEN 821018
    4.    C***REVISION DATE 821018
    5.    C***CATEGORY NO.  D1B1A1
    6.    C***KEYWORDS   ADAPTIVE  QUADRATUE, AUTOMATIC  QUADRATURE
    7.    C***AUTHOR  KAHANER, DAVID K., SCIENTIFIC COMPUTING DIVISION, NBS.
    8.    C***PURPOSE  APPROXIMATES ONE DIMENSIONAL INTEGRALS OF USER DEFINED
    9.    C            FUNCTIONS, EASY TO USE.
   10.    C
   11.    C***DESCRIPTION
   12.    C       Q1DA IS A SUBROUTINE FOR THE AUTOMATIC EVALUATION
   13.    C            OF THE DEFINITE INTEGRAL OF A USER DEFINED FUNCTION
   14.    C            OF ONE VARIABLE.
   15.    C
   16.    C       A R G U M E N T S   I N   T H E   C A L L   S E Q U E N C E
   17.    C
   18.    C       A
   19.    C       B     (INPUT) THE ENDPOINTS OF THE INTEGRATION INTERVAL
   20.    C       EPS   (INPUT) THE ACCURACY TO WHICH YOU WANT THE INTEGRAL
   21.    C                COMPUTED.  IF YOU WANT 2 DIGITS OF ACCURACY SET
   22.    C                EPS=.01, FOR 3 DIGITS SET EPS=.001, ETC.
   23.    C                EPS MUST BE POSITIVE.
   24.    C       R     (OUTPUT) Q1DA'S BEST ESTIMATE OF YOUR INTEGRAL
   25.    C       E     (OUTPUT) AN ESTIMATE OF ABS(INTEGRAL-R)
   26.    C       KF    (OUTPUT) THE COST OF THE INTEGRATION, MEASURED IN
   27.    C                   NUMBER OF EVALUATIONS OF YOUR INTEGRAND.
   28.    C                   KF WILL ALWAYS BE AT LEAST 30.
   29.    C       IFLAG (OUTPUT) TERMINATION FLAG...POSSIBLE VALUES ARE
   30.    C               0   NORMAL COMPLETION, E SATISFIES
   31.    C                        E<EPS  AND  E<EPS*ABS(R)
   32.    C               1   NORMAL COMPLETION, E SATISFIES
   33.    C                        E<EPS, BUT E>EPS*ABS(R)
   34.    C               2   NORMAL COMPLETION, E SATISFIES
   35.    C                        E<EPS*ABS(R), BUT E>EPS
   36.    C               3   NORMAL COMPLETION BUT EPS WAS TOO SMALL TO
   37.    C                     SATISFY ABSOLUTE OR RELATIVE ERROR REQUEST.
   38.    C
   39.    C               4   ABORTED CALCULATION BECAUSE OF SERIOUS ROUNDING
   40.    C                     ERROR.  PROBABLY E AND R ARE CONSISTENT.
   41.    C               5   ABORTED CALCULATION BECAUSE OF INSUFFICIENT STORAGE.
   42.    C                     R AND E ARE CONSISTENT.
   43.    C               6   ABORTED CALCULATION BECAUSE OF SERIOUS DIFFICULTIES
   44.    C                     MEETING YOUR ERROR REQUEST.
   45.    C               7   ABORTED CALCULATION BECAUSE EPS WAS SET <= 0.0
   46.    C
   47.    C            NOTE...IF IFLAG=3, 4, 5 OR 6 CONSIDER USING Q1DAX INSTEAD.
   48.    C
   49.    C    W H E R E   I S   Y O U R   I N T E G R A N D ?
   50.    C
   51.    C        YOU MUST WRITE A FORTRAN FUNCTION, CALLED F, TO EVALUATE
   52.    C        THE INTEGRAND.  USUALLY THIS LOOKS LIKE...
   53.    C                 FUNCTION F(X)
   54.    C                    F=(EVALUATE THE INTEGRAND AT THE POINT X)
   55.    C                    RETURN
   56.    C                 END
   57.    C
   58.    C
   59.    C    T Y P I C A L   P R O B L E M   S E T U P
   60.    C
   61.    C          A=0.0
   62.    C          B=1.0          (SET INTERVAL ENDPOINTS TO [0,1])
   63.    C          EPS=0.001       (SET ACCURACY REQUEST FOR 3 DIGITS)
   64.    C          CALL Q1DA(A,B,EPS,R,E,KF,IFLAG)
   65.    C          END
   66.    C          FUNCTION F(X)
   67.    C            F=SIN(2.*X)-SQRT(X)     (FOR EXAMPLE)
   68.    C            RETURN
   69.    C          END
   70.    C      FOR THIS SAMPLE PROBLEM, THE OUTPUT IS
   71.    C  0.0    1.0     .001    .041406750    .69077E-07    30    0
   72.    C
   73.    C    R E M A R K   I.
   74.    C
   75.    C           A SMALL AMOUT OF RANDOMIZATION IS BUILT INTO THIS PROGRAM.
   76.    C           CALLING Q1DA A FEW TIMES IN SUCCESSION WILL GIVE DIFFERENT
   77.    C           BUT HOPEFULLY CONSISTENT RESULTS.
   78.    C
   79.    C   R E M A R K   II.
   80.    C
   81.    C           THIS ROUTINE IS DESIGNED FOR INTEGRATION OVER A FINITE
   82.    C           INTERVAL.  THUS THE INPUT ARGUMENTS A AND B MUST BE
   83.    C           VALID REAL NUMBERS ON YOUR COMPUTER.  IF YOU WANT TO DO
   84.    C           AN INTEGRAL OVER AN INFINITE INTERVAL SET A OR B OR BOTH
   85.    C           LARGE ENOUGH SO THAT THE INTERVAL [A,B] CONTAINS MOST OF
   86.    C           THE INTEGRAND.  CARE IS NECESSARY, HOWEVER.  FOR EXAMPLE,
   87.    C           TO INTEGRATE EXP(-X*X) ON THE ENTIRE REAL LINE ONE COULD
   88.    C           TAKE A=-20., B=20. OR SIMILAR VALUES TO GET GOOD RESULTS.
   89.    C           IF YOU TOOK A=-1.E10 AND B=+1.E10 TWO BAD THINGS WOULD
   90.    C           OCCUR. FIRST, YOU WILL CERTAINLY GET AN ERROR MESSAGE FROM
   91.    C           THE EXP ROUTINE, AS ITS ARGUMENT IS TOO SMALL.  OTHER
   92.    C           THINGS COULD HAPPEN TOO, FOR EXAMPLE AN UNDERFLOW.
   93.    C           SECOND, EVEN IF THE ARITHMETIC WORKED PROPERLY Q1DA WILL
   94.    C           SURELY GIVE AN INCORRECT ANSWER, BECAUSE ITS FIRST TRY
   95.    C           AT SAMPLING THE INTEGRAND IS BASED ON YOUR SCALING AND
   96.    C           IT IS VERY UNLIKELY TO SELECT EVALUATION POINTS IN THE
   97.    C           INFINITESMALLY SMALL INTERVAL [-20,20] WHERE ALL THE
   98.    C           INTEGRAND IS CONCENTRATED, WHEN A, B ARE SO LARGE.
   99.    C
  100.    C    M O R E   F L E X I B I L I T Y
  101.    C
  102.    C           Q1DA IS AN EASY TO USE DRIVER FOR ANOTHER PROGRAM, Q1DAX.
  103.    C           Q1DAX PROVIDES SEVERAL OPTIONS WHICH ARE NOT AVAILABLE
  104.    C                WITH Q1DA.
  105.    C
  106.    C***REFERENCES  (NONE)
  107.    C***ROUTINES CALLED (Q1DAX)
  108.    C    E N D   O F   D O C U M E N T A T I O N
  109.    C***END PROLOGUE Q1DA
  110.    C
  111.          REAL*8  W(50,6), F
  112.          LOGICAL RST
  113.          INTEGER N_INT
  114.          EXTERNAL F
  115.    C
  116.    C***FIRST EXECUTUTABLE STATEMENT Q1DA
  117.          N_INT=1
  118.          RST = .FALSE.
  119.          NMAX=50
  120.          CALL Q1DAX(F,A,B,EPS,R,E,N_INT,RST,W,NMAX,FMIN,FMAX,KF,IFLAG)
  121.          RETURN
  122.          END

